1
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,268
How I miss Scotland...

2
00:01:13,310 --> 00:01:16,689
and the sea-- the sea.

3
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:20,151
There's nothing like it
in all the Earth--

4
00:01:20,192 --> 00:01:23,279
the salt in your face,
the wind at your back...

5
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:25,740
and all the world before you.

6
00:01:25,782 --> 00:01:29,869
And you're freer than a bird in the air
or a fish in the ocean.

7
00:01:29,911 --> 00:01:32,706
To be free--

8
00:01:32,747 --> 00:01:37,127
I reckon that's why I joined
the Second War to end all wars.

9
00:01:46,887 --> 00:01:51,225
I was at the university studying to be
a teacher when the call to arms occurred.

10
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:55,271
I was only too eager to put aside my studies
for the glory of action.

11
00:01:55,313 --> 00:02:00,318
I stopped reading history
and became a part of it.

12
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:03,905
I joined the proud ranks of
the Argyll & Sutherland Highlanders...

13
00:02:03,947 --> 00:02:06,908
and became Captain Ernest Gordon.

14
00:02:08,577 --> 00:02:12,790
My commanding officer was
Lieutenant Colonel Stuart McLean,

15
00:02:12,831 --> 00:02:16,335
the finest commander
the 93rd Battalion ever had;

16
00:02:16,377 --> 00:02:19,630
a man of deep loyalties
to his country,

17
00:02:19,672 --> 00:02:23,176
his duty and his men;

18
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:27,180
a loyalty that was soon to be matched
by his own second in command,

19
00:02:27,221 --> 00:02:29,224
Major Ian Campbell,

20
00:02:29,265 --> 00:02:33,895
a man of passionate devotion to the colonel
as well as the cause.

21
00:02:36,314 --> 00:02:40,444
And it was our loyalties
that would eventually be tested.

22
00:02:40,485 --> 00:02:44,740
The Argylls had a legacy
or being the last line of defense,

23
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:49,703
and we were to prove
that legacy once again...

24
00:02:49,745 --> 00:02:52,623
in the face of defeat...

25
00:02:52,665 --> 00:02:54,876
and capture by the enemy.

26
00:03:51,227 --> 00:03:54,105
Sir! Sir!

27
00:03:54,146 --> 00:03:56,441
You all right, sir?

28
00:03:56,482 --> 00:04:00,778
You look the devil in the face
with pride, boys!

29
00:04:02,155 --> 00:04:04,908
And try not shit your kilts.

30
00:04:17,046 --> 00:04:19,256
Bastards are just
playing with our minds.

31
00:05:30,039 --> 00:05:32,542
When you surrender in war,

32
00:05:32,584 --> 00:05:35,211
you're stripped of your dignity
as a soldier.

33
00:05:35,253 --> 00:05:38,590
All you've got left
is your fellow comrades,

34
00:05:38,632 --> 00:05:41,718
many of whom you've just met.

35
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:44,638
Lieutenant Jim Reardon,
merchant marine,

36
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,849
one of the few Americans
in the area.

37
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:50,477
Attached himself to the Argylls
during the Allied surrender.

38
00:05:50,519 --> 00:05:54,148
We called him "Yanker,"
'cause he was both an American...

39
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:57,944
- and a bit of a wanker.

40
00:07:05,557 --> 00:07:08,685
Parade, halt.

41
00:07:13,774 --> 00:07:15,817
What's that stench?.

42
00:07:17,486 --> 00:07:19,571
That'll be death, Major.

43
00:07:26,454 --> 00:07:28,498
Sir!!

44
00:07:51,689 --> 00:07:55,401
Parade, right wheel.

45
00:07:59,697 --> 00:08:02,241
Parade, halt.

46
00:08:03,201 --> 00:08:05,745
Left turn.

47
00:08:07,205 --> 00:08:09,499
Stand at ease.

48
00:08:13,795 --> 00:08:15,839
Stand easy.

49
00:08:25,975 --> 00:08:28,936
What the hell
does "tenko" mean?.

50
00:08:28,978 --> 00:08:31,189
Must be roll call.

51
00:08:37,362 --> 00:08:41,157
Today, you will
count off in English.

52
00:08:41,199 --> 00:08:45,203
But tomorrow and forever,
in Japanese.

53
00:09:07,894 --> 00:09:11,314
- Start counting!
- One!

54
00:09:11,356 --> 00:09:13,275
- Two!
- Three!!

55
00:09:13,316 --> 00:09:15,402
- Four!!
- Five.

56
00:09:15,444 --> 00:09:17,279
- Six.
- Big-eared Nip.

57
00:09:21,784 --> 00:09:24,578
Bastard.

58
00:09:36,466 --> 00:09:41,888
This is a shame culture.
You make 'em lose face, you'll pay. Stand easy.

59
00:09:44,266 --> 00:09:46,935
This is an outrage!!

60
00:09:46,977 --> 00:09:49,730
I am a senior officer,

61
00:09:49,772 --> 00:09:53,567
and I will not tolerate
this barbaric behavior!

62
00:09:53,609 --> 00:09:56,445
This man has rights!

63
00:10:09,334 --> 00:10:11,419
Dirty--

64
00:10:39,073 --> 00:10:42,744
Attention, prisoners.
You are now captives...

65
00:10:42,786 --> 00:10:46,248
of His Imperial Majesty,
the emperor.

66
00:10:55,674 --> 00:11:00,805
Behold-- the extreme penalty
for those who try to escape.

67
00:11:24,747 --> 00:11:27,166
This is for your own sake.

68
00:11:28,334 --> 00:11:33,256
You will sign these papers
promising not to escape.

69
00:11:39,179 --> 00:11:43,266
- No escape, my Yankee ass.
- This is against the Geneva Convention.

70
00:11:43,308 --> 00:11:46,228
They don't give two shits
about the Geneva Convention.

71
00:11:46,269 --> 00:11:48,480
Tomorrow,

72
00:11:48,522 --> 00:11:53,318
you will bring these signed papers
from all prisoners.

73
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:55,738
You are dismissed.

74
00:12:40,785 --> 00:12:43,455
Go. Go!

75
00:13:11,401 --> 00:13:14,821
- What did you just say?.
- I told him you were stupid.

76
00:13:15,989 --> 00:13:19,242
Don't know whether
to thank you or kick your ass.

77
00:13:19,284 --> 00:13:21,537
Ian Campbell.

78
00:13:21,578 --> 00:13:23,789
Dusty Miller.

79
00:13:23,831 --> 00:13:26,208
Where'd you learn
to speak Japanese?.

80
00:13:27,459 --> 00:13:29,795
What with the colonel?.

81
00:13:29,837 --> 00:13:32,798
You want your colonel back?.

82
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:35,051
Owe the man my life.

83
00:13:36,094 --> 00:13:39,931
- The more hair, the more lice.
- Ow!

84
00:13:39,973 --> 00:13:44,769
A word of advice--
stay close to your cobbers.

85
00:13:44,811 --> 00:13:48,356
But when it comes right down to it,
it's survival of the fittest.

86
00:13:48,398 --> 00:13:54,154
Every man for himself.
Barter is the name of the game, gents.

87
00:13:54,196 --> 00:13:59,118
They call the hospital the Death House.
You don't want to end up in here.

88
00:14:00,536 --> 00:14:05,917
Relish your health now, gentlemen.
It's the last you’ll see of it.

89
00:14:05,958 --> 00:14:10,588
There are thousands more prisoners
in camps all along the river.

90
00:14:10,630 --> 00:14:14,009
Not too much skinny here
from the outside.

91
00:14:14,050 --> 00:14:16,303
Japs keep us in the dark.

92
00:14:16,344 --> 00:14:18,346
No radios.

93
00:14:18,388 --> 00:14:21,266
They catch one, they'll kill you.

94
00:14:23,268 --> 00:14:27,439
Officers, try to keep
your shirt on.

95
00:14:27,481 --> 00:14:30,359
It'll distinguish you from the grunts,
which is about the only thing...

96
00:14:30,401 --> 00:14:34,906
that's keeping us from degenerating
into a bloody anarchy.

97
00:14:40,453 --> 00:14:42,831
Oh, shit.

98
00:15:03,602 --> 00:15:05,771
Ernie, where ya goin'?.

99
00:15:05,813 --> 00:15:08,566
To find out about the colonel.

100
00:15:08,608 --> 00:15:11,444
You're just askin' for trouble!

101
00:15:36,637 --> 00:15:38,639
Doc.

102
00:15:38,681 --> 00:15:40,642
Get him down here.

103
00:15:44,270 --> 00:15:47,107
- Come on.
- Easy. Easy.

104
00:15:47,148 --> 00:15:49,693
It's my back.
My back, my back.

105
00:15:49,734 --> 00:15:54,114
- Eh.
- What the bloody hell happened?.

106
00:15:54,156 --> 00:15:57,076
- Looks like you didn't bow.
- What?.

107
00:15:57,117 --> 00:16:01,497
Always bow before a guard,
Korean or Jap.

108
00:16:01,539 --> 00:16:04,208
Never look 'em in the eyes
when they pass you.

109
00:16:04,250 --> 00:16:07,295
That's pure defiance.
Always look away. Rules of Bushido.

110
00:16:07,336 --> 00:16:09,839
- Bushido?.